---
const productImageBase = "https://dl.roder.sh/desktop/product";
const panels = [
  { title: "Files & Review", body: "Open project files and inspect changed code beside the thread." },
  { title: "Terminal", body: "Run project commands and inspect their output in a native terminal." },
  { title: "Browser", body: "Open a local preview or let the agent inspect and interact with a page." },
  { title: "Canvas & Design", body: "Sketch, annotate, or work with structured layers, text, and design tokens." },
];
---

<section class="desktop-product-workflows" aria-labelledby="desktop-workflows-heading">
  <div class="desktop-container">
    <header class="desktop-product-workflows-head">
      <p class="desktop-product-eyebrow">From intent to evidence</p>
      <h2 id="desktop-workflows-heading">The work belongs<br />beside the conversation.</h2>
      <p>
        A task spans more than a prompt. Keep the project, delegated work, and
        the result connected as you move from one step to the next.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="desktop-product-project">
      <div class="desktop-product-story-copy">
        <p class="desktop-product-step">01 / Ground the work</p>
        <h3>Start with a project.<br />Keep its history.</h3>
        <p>
          Open a folder and give each task a thread. Return to earlier decisions
          and tool history instead of rebuilding the conversation from scratch.
        </p>
        <a class="desktop-product-link" href="/docs/desktop/">Projects, threads &amp; local issues <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
      </div>
      <dl class="desktop-product-project-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>A place for each task</dt>
          <dd>Project-grouped threads, saved locally. Archive finished project groups and restore them later.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A route back through the work</dt>
          <dd>Navigate long transcripts and reopen earlier turns. Persisted history is separate from the model's context window.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A local task list</dt>
          <dd>Track issues and launch a linked thread in the right workspace. No hosted issue service or automatic external sync.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </article>

    <article class="desktop-product-delegation">
      <figure class="desktop-screenshot-card">
        <img
          src={`${productImageBase}/desktop-subagents-panel.png`}
          alt="Roder Desktop transcript with subagent lifecycle updates and a panel of completed child agents"
          width="1440"
          height="940"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Actual Desktop capture: delegated work has status and a thread you can inspect.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="desktop-product-story-copy">
        <p class="desktop-product-step">02 / Delegate visibly</p>
        <h3>Split the task.<br />Follow the agents.</h3>
        <p>
          Use subagents for independent parts of the job. Lifecycle updates in the
          transcript and the activity overview keep child threads visible as
          they run and finish.
        </p>
        <p>
          Delegation uses the tools and policy available to the thread.
          Review the returned work before accepting it.
        </p>
        <a class="desktop-product-link" href="/docs/agent-control/">How agent control works <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="desktop-product-evidence" aria-labelledby="desktop-evidence-heading">
  <div class="desktop-container desktop-product-evidence-grid">
    <div class="desktop-product-story-copy">
      <p class="desktop-product-step">03 / Inspect the result</p>
      <h2 id="desktop-evidence-heading">See the change.<br />Check the result.</h2>
      <p>
        Keep a diff, a running command, or a browser preview beside the agent.
        Check what happened in the workspace before moving on.
      </p>
      <a class="desktop-product-link" href="/docs/desktop/">Explore the workspace panels <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
    </div>
    <dl class="desktop-product-panel-list">
      {panels.map((panel) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{panel.title}</dt>
          <dd>{panel.body}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="desktop-product-continuity" aria-labelledby="desktop-continuity-heading">
  <div class="desktop-container desktop-product-continuity-grid">
    <article class="desktop-product-mission">
      <p class="desktop-product-step">04 / Keep a mission going</p>
      <h2 id="desktop-continuity-heading">Give repeat work<br />a home.</h2>
      <p>
        A persistent agent keeps a mission, editable memory, and a durable
        thread across check-ins. Use it for ongoing project work that needs
        continuity beyond a single task.
      </p>
      <div class="desktop-product-mission-example">
        <span>Example mission</span>
        <blockquote>Keep this project's documentation aligned with released features.</blockquote>
      </div>
      <p class="desktop-product-note">
        New agents start paused. Enable proactive work explicitly, set run and
        time limits, and keep Desktop running for check-ins to execute.
      </p>
      <a class="desktop-product-link" href="/docs/persistent-agents/">Persistent agents &amp; their controls <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
    </article>
    <article class="desktop-product-execution">
      <p class="desktop-product-eyebrow">Choose where code executes</p>
      <h3>Local by default.<br />Remote when configured.</h3>
      <p>
        On supported platforms, Cloud Machines profiles bind a new thread's
        native coding tools to a configured runner workspace.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prepare the remote repository yourself. Profiles do not sync local
        files, and Desktop's terminal, browser, and file panels remain local.
        The execution binding stays fixed for that thread.
      </p>
      <p class="desktop-product-note">
        Cloud Machines are unavailable on Windows in the current documented release.
        Runner credentials, infrastructure, and charges are separate.
      </p>
      <a class="desktop-product-link" href="/docs/cloud-machines/">Setup &amp; platform limits <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
